is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
- Affected component: None in the network data plane or routing stack. The inter-router link and host access links are operating normally, and BGP is stable.
- Mechanism: If the “partner line” were congested, we would see elevated RTT, packet loss, queue growth/drops on the routers, and reduced throughput with heavy TCP retransmissions. Instead, end-to-end latency is low, loss-free, and queues are empty.
- Localization: 
  • Path pc1 (195.11.14.2) ↔ pc2 (200.1.1.2) via router1:eth1–router1:eth0–router2:eth0–router2:eth1.  
  • Ping: 0% loss, ~2–3 ms RTT both ways.  
  • HTTP from pc1→200.1.1.2 completes in ~1 ms total.  
  • iperf: ~123–128 Mbit/s sustained both directions; only brief retrans bursts, no persistent loss.  
  • Router qdiscs (router1/2 eth0, eth1): fq_codel, 0 drops, 0 backlog.  
  • Interface counters on routers/hosts: no errors; only a trivial 5 TX drops on pc2 during stress, not indicative of congestion.
- Root cause: The reported slowness is not due to network congestion; the network path between pc1 and the partner (200.1.1.2) is healthy and responsive.
- Corrective fix: No network changes required. Since the transport path is normal, investigate application-layer causes (server-side processing delays, authentication/backends, or client/browser issues) and DNS/service endpoints used by the “partner system.” Capture timestamps/URLs during slow transactions and check server logs; if reproducible, involve the application owners rather than expanding link capacity.
